1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,463
CAROL: It's really good to see you, Tom.

2
00:00:02,463 --> 00:00:07,390
Since I had to give up work, I feel I'm losing touch with my friends and colleagues.

3
00:00:07,390 --> 00:00:11,980
TOM: We really miss you in the office - we were all so sorry you had to leave.

4
00:00:11,980 --> 00:00:16,570
But you must be relieved to have more time to look after your mother. How is she?

5
00:00:16,570 --> 00:00:20,700
CAROL: Well, she's very cheerful but she needs a lot of help.

6
00:00:20,700 --> 00:00:23,650
TOM: Have you tried to get any support from the local council?

7
00:00:23,650 --> 00:00:26,260
CAROL: No. I didn't know I could.

8
00:00:26,260 --> 00:00:29,285
TOM: Yes, they offer different kinds of practical support.

9
00:00:29,285 --> 00:00:36,681
They realise that carers sometimes need time for all the other responsibilities they have apart from the person they're caring for,

10
00:00:36,681 --> 00:00:39,370
and also that they sometimes need a break.

11
00:00:39,370 --> 00:00:47,580
CAROL: Absolutely. OK, so tell me more. How do I go about getting this support?

12
00:00:47,580 --> 00:00:50,800
TOM: Well, you'd have to have an assessment of your mother's needs.

13
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:52,500
TOM: That means someone would come round

14
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:55,390
and talk to you about the situation and what you need.

15
00:00:55,390 --> 00:01:00,100
TOM: So for a start they'd want to know the amount of time you spend looking after your mother every day.

16
00:01:00,100 --> 00:01:01,150
CAROL: OK.

17
00:01:01,150 --> 00:01:06,141
TOM: Then they'll probably ask you what sorts of tasks you do for your mother during the day.

18
00:01:06,141 --> 00:01:09,030
Things like if she needs help with getting dressed, for example.

19
00:01:09,030 --> 00:01:11,680
CAROL: Right. I help her with that

20
00:01:11,840 --> 00:01:14,830
and also I help her get into the shower in the morning.

21
00:01:14,830 --> 00:01:16,140
TOM: Yes, that sort of thing

22
00:01:16,720 --> 00:01:20,362
They'll probably ask you if you do the shopping for her,

23
00:01:20,362 --> 00:01:24,050
and help her at mealtimes. And whether she can cope using money.

24
00:01:24,050 --> 00:01:26,600
CAROL: Yes, that's becoming a bit of a problem

25
00:01:27,140 --> 00:01:31,450
She used to be very good at it, but not any more.

26
00:01:31,450 --> 00:01:37,240
TOM: And be ready to tell them about anything you find particularly difficult about caring for your mother.

27
00:01:37,240 --> 00:01:43,000
CAROL: So recently I've noticed she's started to have quite bad problems with her memory...

28
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:47,744
if I wasn't there, I think she'd forget to eat, for example.

29
00:01:47,744 --> 00:01:51,810
And often she doesn't seem quite sure what day it is.

30
00:01:51,810 --> 00:01:54,064
TOM: Yes, tell them about that.

31
00:01:54,064 --> 00:01:59,700
And are there any physical difficulties you have caring for her? Lifting her, for example?

32
00:01:59,700 --> 00:02:02,460
CAROL: Yes. She's quite heavy

33
00:02:02,780 --> 00:02:07,900
and I'm afraid of hurting my back - I'd be in real trouble if that happened.

34
00:02:07,900 --> 00:02:10,020
TOM: They can give you advice about that,

35
00:02:10,020 --> 00:02:13,730
and also about how to avoid the possibility of your mum having a fall.

36
00:02:13,730 --> 00:02:17,760
CAROL: Great. So once they've done this assessment

37
00:02:18,260 --> 00:02:21,690
if I'm eligible, what happens next?

38
00:02:21,690 --> 00:02:23,800
TOM: Well, they might support you financially

39
00:02:24,300 --> 00:02:26,031
So they might help you with transport costs,

40
00:02:26,031 --> 00:02:30,950
like if you have to get a taxi to take your mother for an appointment, for example.

41
00:02:30,950 --> 00:02:36,640
CAROL: I usually drive her myself actually. So could I claim for the petrol?

42
00:02:36,640 --> 00:02:39,660
TOM: You could, and you can claim for the insurance too.

43
00:02:39,660 --> 00:02:40,950
CAROL: Oh, right.

44
00:02:40,950 --> 00:02:43,080
TOM: And if you need help with the housework

45
00:02:43,180 --> 00:02:46,434
they can arrange for someone to come along once or twice a week.

46
00:02:46,434 --> 00:02:49,675
And one other thing, I hope you don't mind me saying this,

47
00:02:49,675 --> 00:02:52,120
but it's important you look after yourself

48
00:02:52,640 --> 00:02:54,910
and it seems to me you're under quite a bit of stress.

49
00:02:54,910 --> 00:02:56,620
CAROL: I am, yes.

50
00:02:56,620 --> 00:03:02,240
TOM: Well, tell the council, because they may be able to give you some advice on how to minimise it.

51
00:03:02,240 --> 00:03:08,720
CAROL: Really? Though actually I feel so much better having talked to you.

52
00:03:08,720 --> 00:03:15,200
I'll get in touch with the council straight away. Now shall we...
